#X obj 131 360 line;
#X obj 26 340 delay 500;
#X text 554 141 Any programming language that I have come across has a delay type "thing" in it. I just typed in "delay" into the object box \, then checked the properties on it. The way I am using the [delay] is simple. 500 gives 500ms after it has been sent its [bang]. 500ms is certainly not needed \, however \, it gives the user lots of time between the click of the [bang] and seeing the end result of the number change.;
#X msg 150 328 0 \, 0.001 2500;
#X text 550 265 I left the use of the slowly increasing envelope in place to remind myself that the other [bang] is being triggered even though the envelope does not reach its full value of 1 after the 1500ms. This tells be the bang can be triggered by any non-zero number. I can test that by reducinc the target value in the message box feeding into [line]. And then it is interesting that the [bang] continues to be held for the length of the envelope. If you increase the time it takes to reach the target \, even with the target very low \, the [bang] being targeted stays activated for the length of the envelope. I suppose you could use this envelope setup as sort of a reverse gate. Have something triggered when the [bang] turns off.;
